<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jinheung of Silla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">526–576</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jinheung was king of Silla from 540 to 576 and its greatest early conqueror. Allied with Baekje, he took the Han river valley from Goguryeo and then seized it from Baekje in 553, gaining access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-sea">Yellow Sea</a>. He annexed Daegaya and the remaining Gaya states in 562, erected boundary steles recording his tours, and founded the hwarang youth corps.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jinheung was the 24th monarch of Silla, one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-of-korea">Three Kingdoms of Korea</a>. His father was Galmunwang Ipjong, a son of King Jijeung, and his mother was Queen Jiso, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beopheung-of-silla">King Beopheung</a>, so that Beopheung, whom he succeeded, was both his uncle and his grandfather. He came to the throne as a child, and his mother ruled as regent until he came of age; the change of era name in 551 marks the start of his own rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 541 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabu">Kim Isabu</a> was made head of military affairs, and he played a large part in Jinheung's conquests. In 551 Jinheung allied with Baekje against Goguryeo, and the two kingdoms took the Han river estuary and agreed to share it equally.</p><p>In 553, under a secret agreement with Goguryeo, Silla troops turned on the exhausted Baekje army, although Jinheung had married a daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seong-of-baekje">Seong of Baekje</a> as a lesser consort. When Seong attacked in 554, he was caught in an ambush led by a Silla general and killed. Jinheung then sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabu">Kim Isabu</a> to conquer Daegaya, set up monuments and provinces in the new lands, and in 576 established the Hwarang, young warriors who studied arts and music as well as fighting.</p><p>Access to the Han valley gave Silla direct contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, from which it acquired Buddhist relics. Jinheung was a devout Buddhist who saw himself as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chakravarti-sanskrit-term">Chakravarti</a>, the ideal universal ruler; temples such as Hwangnyongsa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju">Gyeongju</a> were founded and monks were given roles at court. He also ordered the compilation of a history of Silla, the Guksa, which is lost but appears to have been a source for the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-sagi">Samguk sagi</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Jinheung died in 576 after a reign of conquest and advancement. As Crown Prince Dongryun had died in 572, he was succeeded by his second son, Prince Saryun, who became King Jinji. His achievements laid the basis for the later unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, and he is remembered today by the Korean people as one of the most important rulers of Silla.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jinheung of Silla” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>King <b>Jinheung</b> (r. 540–576) allied with Baekje to drive Goguryeo from the Han River valley in 551, then seized Baekje's share in 553, gaining access to the Yellow Sea and direct contact with China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/silla">Silla · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Silla established a province at Bisabeol in 555 and <b>King Jinheung</b> erected a stele there in 561 recording his tour and naming his officials, marking the area's full incorporation.</blockquote><a href="/state/bihwa-gaya">Bihwa Gaya</a></li><li><blockquote>Silla absorbed the states one by one: Geumgwan Gaya surrendered in 532 and Daegaya fell to <b>King Jinheung</b> in 562.</blockquote><a href="/state/gaya-confederacy">Gaya confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju">Gyeongju</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.85,129.22&amp;year=558" aria-label="Gyeongju on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Jinheung of Silla"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5636 4475h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5636 4475h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.85,129.22&amp;year=558">What was here in 558 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
